\f1\fs22 The three Sages returned to the laboratory with Chip reluctantly in tow. Sarah had insisted the former Fridge Force agent attend this \'93important consultation\'94; he was the only person in the building with enough practical experience to recognise the difference between \'93breakthrough\'94 and \'93catastrophic incident report.\'94 Chip had exhausted his excuses three meetings ago. They found Johnny standing before a significantly altered testing apparatus, dark circles under his eyes suggesting he\'92d achieved that peculiar state of exhaustion where the human body begins to resemble a poorly maintained houseplant.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The temporal matrix calibration was a stroke of genius,\'94 Johnny admitted, which was Johnny\'92s way of saying someone on his team had actually solved a problem while he was asleep at his desk. \'93I thought of that.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo tittered but Johnny was too excited by progress to maintain his usual veneer of superiority. \'93Look at this.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The holographic display now showed not just the oscillating patterns that had haunted Sarah\'92s dreams, but what appeared to be script\'97hundreds of lines of strange symbols rotating in three-dimensional space\'97multiple writing systems, none of them decipherable, all of them ominous.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny continued, \'93It\'92s definitely language\'97or rather, languages. From different civilisations, possibly across the multiverse.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo circled the display with the practiced nonchalance of a man who\'92d spent several millennia pretending not to know things he absolutely did know. \'93The next step would be to attempt to activate the translations,\'94 he said as if uttering a random thought that had just occurred to him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny frowned, then tried to cover for his inability to understand a single thing the Sages said. \'93Yes, of course. It\'92s on the roadmap. Right now, I\'92m isolating a referential baseline for deriving translation algorithms.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Sages hid their smirks behind hands and florid sleeve cuffs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo nodded sympathetically. \'93Though in my studies of ancient transmission systems, I\'92ve found that sometimes the key is hidden in the medium itself.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos coughed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip added, \'93But then, the risks\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What risks?\'94 Johnny\'92s tone sharpened. \'93What key?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Rotherhithe Stasis Collapse,\'94 Chip said, and hated himself for saying it out loud, because now it was in the room and therefore immediately eligible to be dismissed. \'93Earthquakes, continental lightning storms, seas boiling. That kind of thing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What, so just the usual apocalyptic promises?\'94 Johnny challenged. \'93I\'92ve personally been working on all the safeguards,\'94 he added, which roughly translated to: 
\f2\i A junior team somewhere whose names I don\'92t recall are working on it, possibly in a building I\'92ve never visited.
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip was about to object, but sublimated his fears into a heaving sigh from his bear-sized chest. No one was listening to him anyway. He\'92d spent enough time in corporate hierarchies to know that being right was worth precisely nothing if you were the wrong level of seniority. He resolved that rather than playing the role of the constant doom-sayer\'97and thereby having his every thought dismissed as background noise from the professionally paranoid\'97he needed to time his run, wait for the evidence to mount. He imagined the meeting where he\'92d triumphantly announce, \'93I really thought this could work, but...\'94 and then lay out the incontrovertible proof while watching Johnny\'92s face collapse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We, the Seven Sages\'97\'94 Kleoboulos began.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93All three of you,\'94 Chip interjected.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos nodded, as if he had all the time in the world, which he probably did. \'93Ancient writings suggest that energies of the kind emitted by Gyges Discs in certain configurations may do more than just channel messages\'97they may be designed to transmit them.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip pulled out his tablet and hovered an index finger over its surface. \'93Could you give me a link to where we can read these ancient writings?\'94 He immediately regretted being drawn into a confrontation with people who looked old enough to have been having arguments since before written language was invented.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos shrugged enigmatically. \'93Like most arcana, young Mr Stockwell, they require certain esoteric perspectives to interpret.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip grunted and held his powder dry. He\'92d learned that arguing with mystics was like arguing with HR\'97you never won, you just delayed the inevitable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So you\'92re suggesting we could use the discs to communicate with other universes?\'94 Johnny asked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Theoretically,\'94 Solo replied.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Good, because I\'92d already thought of that,\'94 Johnny said with the confidence of someone who had absolutely not thought of that.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You have?\'94 Chilo asked and smiled in such a way that Johnny thought he was either delighted or was about to eat him raw.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, yes,\'94 Johnny stalled while his mind raced, by which we mean it ran at a cognitive load only slightly greater than doing a junior crossword puzzle. \'93But we need to experiment with other configurations,\'94 he improvised. \'93We need more... Gyges Discs.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip almost choked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s brilliant insight,\'94 Chilo observed with the sincerity of a used car salesman complimenting a mark\'92s taste in transport.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yes, it is rather, isn\'92t it?\'94 Johnny blathered and exhaled in relief. Yet again, his genius had pulled a multi-dimensional rabbit out of his singularly dimensioned arse. But it was obvious, wasn\'92t it? If two Gyges Discs were better than one, then surely any number of Gyges Discs would be better than two. This was basic mathematics, the kind of insight that separated visionaries from mere technicians.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Johnny nodded slowly, less from confidence than a forlorn desire to entrain his brain to keep producing the kind of brilliance it had just gifted him with. He performed rapid calculations about how such a breakthrough would transform his reputation\'97calculations that involved more finger-counting than he\'92d care to admit and absolutely no actual mathematics.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Dr. Quark has been reluctant to increase power to the discs after what happened in initial testing, but it\'92s all just a matter of configuration and stability.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Dr. Quark is prudent,\'94 Chilo conceded. \'93And yet, prudence produces nothing new. No new insight, no new connections.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93No new glory,\'94 Solo added for Johnny\'92s benefit. While he and the other Sages had their disagreements about tactics, timing, and whether Kleoboulos\'92s beard made him look distinguished or simply homeless, they all knew Johnny was the perfect instrument for enabling their agenda. He moved closer to the control panel, pointing to settings without actually touching them\'97the gesture of a man who understood that touching things implied responsibility for outcomes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93With three Gyges Discs,\'94 he said in the tone of someone reciting scripture they didn\'92t quite understand, \'93the harmonic resonance between your phase variance compensator and the discs in the right configuration might stabilise a feedback loop that would amplify the signal without requiring dangerous external power inputs.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip made a noise somewhere between a laugh and coughing up seawater. \'93That\'92s exactly what caused the Rotherhithe Stasis Collapse!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Come now, Chip,\'94 Johnny was basting in his own smugness like a self-satisfied turkey. \'93We\'92re talking about components found in every stasis unit in the land. Aren\'92t you being over-dramatic?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chip gulped and silently cursed. This was exactly what he was afraid of: being dismissed as a broken record of doom, the Cassandra of the laboratory, the man who cried apocalypse so often that when the actual apocalypse arrived, everyone would assume he was just having another episode. And yet he couldn\'92t stop himself. Some part of him still believed that if he found the right words, the right tone, the right amount of not-sounding-like-a-man-who\'92d-seen-too-much, he might make a difference.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It violates at least seventeen sections of the Food Preservation Secrets Act,\'94 he said weakly, deploying regulatory compliance as a shield against catastrophe.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Ah, so your objections are administrative, based on politics rather than physics,\'94 Johnny asserted triumphantly, as if the laws of bureaucracy and physics existed in entirely separate magisteria, and violating one had no bearing on the other. \'93Very well then, Mr Stockwell. How about four discs?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Or five,\'94 Chilo encouraged, delighted at the direction the meeting was taking\'97checkmate in three moves, and Johnny couldn\'92t see any of them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh, Jesus mother-taxing Mary,\'94 Chip cursed and made for the door. He didn\'92t even bother pretending he had a plan anymore. \'93I need to talk with Ms Jacobson.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93She won\'92t help you,\'94 Johnny called after him. \'93You need to understand whose pockets your superiors are in.\'94 The smug smile again, the kind that made people fantasise about violence. \'93Sarah and I have been the best of intimates for years!\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A silence fell over the room with the weight and texture of an unwelcome revelation at a family gathering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Very good then.\'94 Chilo anticipated closing the meeting before anyone could process what Johnny had just said. \'93We are in agreement? A configuration of five Gyges Discs?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo nodded as if weighing the proposal with great scholarly consideration.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleoboulos solemnly looked at the floor. \'93It is as the prophecy predicted: 
\f2\i And the power of the light shall be bestowed upon the keepers of time and space in the likeness of a pentagram.
\f1\i0 \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Such a profound moment of stillness and silence fell upon the room that even Kleoboulos\'92s tinnitus gave him a brief reprieve.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93
\f2\i The keepers of time and space
\f1\i0 ,\'94 Johnny repeated while nodding, his chest swelling with the kind of pride that precedes most historical catastrophes. He straightened as if he was an Olympic athlete receiving their first gold medal rather than a middle manager who\'92d just been manipulated into potentially destroying civilisation. \'93I like the sound of that. I think I might put it on my voicemail greeting.\'94
